                        %mo ∨
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      i_! Th\left(Q_{i^\ast \chi}\right) \xi
                      \\colon
                      \\mathbb{C}
                      \\stackrel{\xi}{\to}
                      C_{i^\ast \chi}(Q)
                      \\stackrel{Th}{\to}
                      (C_{i^\ast \chi}(Q))^\vee
                      \\stackrel{i_!}{\to}
                      (C_{\chi}(X))^\vee
                      \\,.
              %p This we regard as the quantization of the boundary correspondence.
          %p (…)
          %h2#2d_poisson_chernsimons_theory__idea 2d Poisson Chern-Simons theory – Idea
          %h3#general General
          %p
            We discuss aspects of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+geometric+quantization"} extended geometric quantization
            of one of the simplest and yet interesting examples of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theory"} ∞-Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            and here specifically the case induced by a binary and non-degenerate
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integrated
            version of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
          %p
            It turns out that several aspects of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+geometric+quantization"} extended geometric quantization
            of this 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            are known in the literature already, albeit in disguise: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-plectic+geometry"} 2-plectic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            is equivalently what in the literature is known as a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
            is essentially what is known as the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
          %p
            The suggestion that there should be such a relation is contained already in (
            %a{:href => "symplectic%20groupoid#CattaneoFelder01"}> Cattaneo-Felder 01
            ), but at the time of that writing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
            had not been performed yet.
          %p
            To some extent in the following we review the traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
            while showing at the same time how its ingredients are (more) naturally interpreted in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+symplectic+geometry"} higher symplectic geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
          %p
            Specifically, we discuss what should be the first nontrivial case of the Chern-Simons-type
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            realized in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            associated to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2-1) = 1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-vector+space"} 2-vector space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+states"} of quantum 2-states
            (assigned to the point n codimension 2). Under the identification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-vector+spaces"} 2-vector spaces
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
            over an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            = succeed "-class" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
            of that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Hawkins08"}> Hawkins (2008)
            \&amp;rbrack;.
          %p
            Notice that at the level of just infinitesimal (“formal”)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            a similar
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic
            relation between quantization of a Poisson manifold and of its associated 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            QFT has famously been shown by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Cattaneo"} Alberto Cattaneo
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giovanni+Felder"} Giovanni Felder
            to underly
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kontsevich"} Kontsevich
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
          %p
            All of this should be a blueprint for an analogous situaton in one dimension higher, where the analogous procedure should reproduce the famous holographic quantization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Wess-Zumino-Witten+theory"} 2d Wess-Zumino-Witten theory
            in terms of that of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
          %h3#identifying_symplectic_groupoids_as_2plectic_groupoids Identifying “symplectic groupoids” as 2-plectic groupoids
          %p
            We briefly indicate the basis for re-interpreting traditional
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+symplectic+geometry"} higher symplectic geometry
          %p
            The identification of the traditional notion of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"}> symplectic groupoid
            ” as really a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-plectic+geometry"} 2-plectic
            structure is evident as soon as one translates the traditional definition of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            to more intrinsic language of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} higher differential geometry
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{X},\omega)
            is traditionally defined to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{X}
            which is equipped with a (non-degenerate)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in \Omega^2(\mathbf{X}_1)
            on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
            as well as by the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            that sums the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+differential+forms"} pullback
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            to the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{X}_2
            of composable morphisms along the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target"} target
            maps and minus that along the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            map. But together this just means that regarded as a triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %munder
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %mo −
                      %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0, \omega, 0) \in \underset{k = 0,1,2}{\oplus} \Omega^{3-k}(\mathbf{X}_k)
            the symplectic form is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+de+Rham+complex"} simplicial de Rham complex
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+manifold"} simplicial manifold
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of the Lie groupoid. And this finally means fully intrinsically that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is a degree-3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            in the
            %a{:href => "cohesive+%28infinity,1%29-topos+--+structures#deRhamCohomology"} intrinsic de Rham cohomology
            %a{:href => "smooth+infinity-groupoid+--+structures#deRhamCoefficientsInBnU1"} of smooth ∞-groupoids
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{X}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mo ♭
                    %mi dR
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 3
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega \;\colon\; \mathbf{X} \to \flat_{dR} \mathbf{B}^3 U(1)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This simple observation shows that
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoids"} symplectic groupoids
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+symplectic+geometry"} higher symplectic geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-plectic+geometry"} 2-plectic geometry
          %p
            More specifically, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\pi)
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔓
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic Lie n-algebroid
            and as such it carries a canonical binary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            of what is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of this data is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+Lagrangian"} extended Lagrangian
            of an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theory"} ∞-Chern-Simons theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi L
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mn 1
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Γ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{L}
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\tau_1\exp(\mathfrak{P})_{conn}
                  \\to
                  \\mathbf{B}^2 (\mathbb{R}/\Gamma)_{conn}
          %p
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            to that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+2-bundles+with+connection"} circle 2-bundles with connection
          %p
            If we
            %span.newWikiWord
              concretify
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/concretify"} ?
            the moduli stack by forgetting the connection data here and just consider the underlying
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sectors"} instanton sectors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi τ
                            %mn 1
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔓
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi conn
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi L
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi Γ
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi conn
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi concretify
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi ω
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ♭
                            %mi dR
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mn 3
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\tau_1\exp(\mathfrak{P})_{conn}
                  \&amp;\stackrel{\mathbf{L}}{\to}&amp;
                  \\mathbf{B}^2 (\mathbb{R}/\Gamma)_{conn}
                  \\\
                  {}^{\mathllap{concretify}}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{X} &amp;\stackrel{\omega}{\to}&amp; \flat_{dR} \mathbf{B}^3 U(1)
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            It is in this way that we may identify the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            of a Poisson manifold
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi X
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mn 1
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{X} \simeq \tau_1 \exp(\mathfrak{P})
          %p
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+prequantum+field+theory"} extended prequantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            which refines the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
          %p
            Hence we identify the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{X}, \omega)
            as the extended geometric quantization of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoids"} symplectic groupoids
            in the first place) that this encodes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \pi)
            itself, regarded as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
          %p
            Taken together this says: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+geometric+quantization"} extended geometric quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+prequantum+field+theory"} extended prequantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            computes the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of the underlying Poisson manifold.
          %p
            This statement we recognize as a geometric quantization analog of a famous relation in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+deformation+quantization"} algebraic deformation quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kontsevich"} Kontsevich
            of the algebraic deformation quantization of any Poisson manifold was identified by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cattaneo"} Cattaneo
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felder"} Felder
            as a limiting case of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+function"} 3-point function
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbative quantization
            of the corresponding 2d Poisson
            = succeed "-model." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            These relations to traditional theory we use in the following to explore aspects of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+geometric+quantization"} extended geometric quantization
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
